# Architecture

The shape of `backend/`: an async Python 3.15 backend on FastAPI{% if include_postgres %}, PostgreSQL{% endif %} and external HTTP APIs, with typed logs, a typer CLI and uv, running as Docker Compose. Each business domain is one package, every external system has one thin layer, and every process (the API, a worker, a CLI command) opens its resources once and passes them down.

The layout follows the per-domain package structure of [fastapi-best-practices](https://github.com/zhanymkanov/fastapi-best-practices), [Polar](https://github.com/polarsource/polar/tree/main/server/polar) and [Netflix Dispatch](https://github.com/Netflix/dispatch/tree/master/src/dispatch), with the repository and service layers from Cosmic Python ([repository](https://www.cosmicpython.com/book/chapter_02_repository.html), [service layer](https://www.cosmicpython.com/book/chapter_04_service_layer.html)).

## The layers

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    entry["Entry points: router.py, cli.py, worker loops"] --> service["service.py (the domain API)"]
    service --> other["lower domains' service.py and read types"]
{%- if include_postgres %}
    service --> repo["repository.py (Postgres), API clients"]
    repo --> infra["Infrastructure: app/infrastructure/ (postgres, rate_limit, &lt;name&gt;_api)"]
{%- else %}
    service --> infra["Infrastructure: app/infrastructure/ (rate_limit, &lt;name&gt;_api clients)"]
{%- endif %}
    lifespan["app/lifespan: Resources and wiring"] -.opens.-> infra
    lifespan -.passes Resources and services.-> entry
```

1. Infrastructure, bound to no domain: {% if include_postgres %}the database layer, {% endif %}the rate limiter, and one package per external API (`app/infrastructure/<name>_api/`).
2. `app/shared/`: domain-free building blocks only (the `AppError` base, the error envelope, logging setup, request helpers{% if include_postgres %}, the named blocking executor{% endif %}). `app/configuration.py` beside it holds the app-wide settings.
3. Domain packages, one per business concept, under `app/{{ root_package }}/`, in one acyclic graph.
4. `app/lifespan/`: `Resources`, one lifespan per process role, and the wiring between domains.
5. Entry points that only translate input and output: HTTP routers, CLI commands, worker loops.

The layers are folders, so the tree reads like the diagram:

```
app/
  main.py cli.py   process entry points
  lifespan/        Resources, one lifespan per process role, composition
  shared/          domain-free building blocks
  infrastructure/  {% if include_postgres %}postgres/ {% endif %}rate_limit/ <name>_api/
  {{ root_package }}/  one package per domain{% if include_accounts %}: accounts/ ...{% endif %}
```

Inside `infrastructure/` and `{{ root_package }}/` every package is flat: `{{ root_package }}/invoices/` beside `{{ root_package }}/accounts/`, never nested. A big domain splits into sibling packages. Routes and CLI commands stay inside their domain (`router.py` beside `service.py`), so one feature lives in one folder.

Dependencies point down only. A domain never imports a router. Infrastructure never imports a domain. A domain may import a lower domain's service and read types, never in a cycle. import-linter enforces this (`lint-imports`, `[tool.importlinter]` in `backend/pyproject.toml`): a `layers` contract over entry points, middlewares, lifespan, domains, infrastructure and shared, whose domain order also proves there are no cycles between domains{% if include_accounts %}, and a `forbidden` contract that keeps routers, dependencies, CLI commands and entry modules away from every `models` and `repository` module{% endif %}. A new domain takes its place in the `layers` list.

## Data stores
{% if include_postgres %}
PostgreSQL is the only store: the application's own tables, and the source of truth.

A domain owns its tables. Other domains read them through its service, never with their own SQL. A reference to another domain's row is an id. There is no ORM relationship across domains.
{%- else %}
The app keeps no state of its own. It has no database, and the request budgets live in the API process's memory, so a restart clears them.
{%- endif %}

## A domain package

```
app/{{ root_package }}/<domain>/
{%- if include_postgres %}
  models.py          SQLAlchemy models for the tables this domain owns
{%- endif %}
  schemas.py         Pydantic request and response bodies, only when the domain has a router
  types.py           read types other domains may use (frozen models)
  enums.py           closed value sets shared with other domains
  errors.py          AppError subclasses, only when callers need to tell them apart
  service.py         <Domain>Service: use cases, the only thing other code calls
{%- if include_postgres %}
  repository.py      queries for this domain's own tables, no decisions
{%- endif %}
  router.py          HTTP routes, thin
  dependencies.py    request-scoped injection, only when a router needs it
  sweeps.py          clock-driven passes a worker loop runs, only when the domain has any
  cli.py             CLI commands for this domain, only when it has any
  configuration.py   pydantic-settings for this domain, only when it has settings
  log_events.py      typed log records
```

A file exists only when something runs, opens, injects, configures or raises through it. An empty file is noise. There is no catch-all `adapters.py`, `utils.py` or `helpers.py`; a file is named after what it implements.

{%- if include_postgres %}
- **Repository:** data access for this domain's tables (`find_account()`, `insert_session()`). Queries only; it decides nothing.
{%- endif %}
- **Service:** the use cases. It calls {% if include_postgres %}its own repository, {% endif %}API clients and lower domains' services. When it grows, split it by use case into `<feature>_service.py` files inside the domain. It never grows a new layer.
- **Models:** the package that owns the {% if include_postgres %}table or the {% endif %}API owns the model. Other packages never redefine it.

Rules and the reasons for them:

{%- if include_postgres %}
- Code outside the domain calls `service.py`, never the repository. The service is the domain's public API, so storage can move without touching callers.
- Other domains name a row by a read type from `types.py`. The ORM model stays inside its domain.
{%- else %}
- Code outside the domain calls `service.py`. The service is the domain's public API.
- Other domains read a domain's data through a read type from `types.py`.
{%- endif %}
- The service receives what it needs through its constructor, by keyword{% if include_accounts %}: `AccountService(repository=..., relying_party=...)`{% endif %}. It never reads `app.state` or module globals, so it works the same in a request, a worker loop and a CLI command. {% if include_postgres %}A `classmethod` such as `on_session(session)` builds it for the common case.{% else %}A `classmethod` builds it for the common case.{% endif %}
- A service returns typed models. A tuple or dict return is a missing model: a two-part result gets a model with two fields.
- The service owns domain decisions. Routers and CLI commands parse input, call one service method, and shape the output or error.

### Calling another domain

Down the graph, a service takes the lower domain's service through its constructor. The wiring is written once, in `app/lifespan/`, and routers and CLI commands take services from there.

Up the graph, prefer not to. Two domains that need each other share a third, higher domain that owns the orchestration.

{%- if include_postgres %}

## Postgres

`app/infrastructure/postgres/` holds `Database` (async engine and session factory, with statement, lock and idle-in-transaction timeouts set per connection), the ORM `Base`, the `SurrogateId` and `Timestamps` mixins (a `bigint` identity id; `created_at`, `updated_at` as `timestamptz`) and `build_postgres_enum`.

A repository takes an `AsyncSession` by keyword and holds no business rules:

```python
class AccountRepository:
    def __init__(self, *, session: AsyncSession) -> None:
        self.session = session

    async def find_account(self, account_id: int) -> Account | None:
        return await self.session.get(Account, account_id)
```

Queries name their columns where they select partial rows; never string-format SQL.

### Database roles

The postgres image makes `POSTGRES_USER` a superuser, so the app has two roles:

- **`{{ database_name }}`**, the image's superuser, owns every object. Only `migrate`, the backup scripts and the operator connect as it.
- **`{{ database_name }}_application`**, the API's role (and any worker's), is `NOSUPERUSER NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE NOREPLICATION NOBYPASSRLS`. It has `CONNECT` on the database, `USAGE` on schema `public`, `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE` on its tables and `USAGE, SELECT` on its sequences. It can't create, alter or drop anything: "In PostgreSQL 15 and later, the default configuration" keeps `CREATE` on `public` from `PUBLIC` ([schemas](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-schemas.html#DDL-SCHEMAS-PATTERNS)), and schemas carry no other default grants ([privileges](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-priv.html)). Without superuser it can't run `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` or read server files.

Alembic revision `0003` creates the role when it is missing and grants on the tables and sequences that exist, `alembic_version` among them, which the API's schema check reads. `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE {{ database_name }}` covers every table and sequence a later revision creates; it applies only to objects "created in the future" by that role ([ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-alterdefaultprivileges.html)), which is why the existing ones get their own `GRANT`. A revision that creates something in another schema, or a function the API calls, grants on it itself.

The role lives in a revision rather than a `/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d` script because that script runs only on an empty data directory, so an app updated over an existing volume would never get the role. Roles are cluster-wide, so a WAL-G restore brings the role back with the base backup either way. A revision can't hold a password, so `migrations/env.py` sets it after every upgrade from `{{ environment_prefix }}_APPLICATION_DATABASE_PASSWORD`: it hashes the password to a SCRAM verifier on the client (libpq's `PQencryptPasswordConn`) and sends only the verifier, so the password never reaches a server log or a command line. `migrate` reads that key from the API's env file, so rotating it is a change to `.env` and a deploy.

### Units of work

One session is one transaction is one unit of work: a request, a worker pass or a CLI command. The entry point opens the session and commits or rolls back (`DatabaseSession` in `app/lifespan/dependencies.py` for a request). Services and repositories only `flush`, so two repositories called by one service write atomically. A transaction never spans two databases.

A write that must be serialised across processes takes `pg_advisory_xact_lock` on a named key at the start of its transaction, so the lock ends with the transaction.

### Pool budget

Each process holds its own pool, and the sum over every process stays under the server's limit. `max_connections` is 100, set explicitly in `docker-compose.yml` (its default is "typically 100" and it "can only be set at server start", [connections](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-connection.html#GUC-MAX-CONNECTIONS)). The API's pool is `API_DATABASE_POOL_SIZE` 10 plus `API_DATABASE_MAXIMUM_OVERFLOW` 5, so it holds at most 15 (`app/lifespan/composition.py`). Add every other process at its own maximum: each worker's pool, the `migrate` run, a CLI command, and the backup scripts' `psql` and `wal-g` sessions. The total stays under 100 minus `superuser_reserved_connections` (3). The API's role is no superuser, so a runaway pool can't take those 3 slots from the operator. Prefer a smaller pool to a higher `max_connections`: the server sizes shared memory by it, and every extra connection can claim its own `work_mem`.

## Postgres operations

### Server settings

The `postgres` service starts the server with explicit `-c` flags in `docker-compose.yml`, so every environment runs the same settings and `SHOW <setting>` proves what is live. Every value below was checked against the PostgreSQL 18 manual.

| Setting | Value | PG18 default | Why |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `shared_preload_libraries` | `pg_stat_statements` | empty | The module "must be loaded by adding `pg_stat_statements` to `shared_preload_libraries`", because it needs shared memory; a change needs a restart ([pg_stat_statements](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/pgstatstatements.html)). Query ids are computed because `compute_query_id` defaults to `auto`, which lets the module turn it on ([statistics](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-statistics.html#GUC-COMPUTE-QUERY-ID)). Alembic revision `0002` runs `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_stat_statements`, since the view exists only in a database that created the extension. |
| `max_connections` | `100` | typically 100 | See Pool budget. |
| `shared_buffers` | `POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS`, default `256MB` | `128MB` | "A reasonable starting value for `shared_buffers` is 25% of the memory in your system", and above 40% is unlikely to help ([resources](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-SHARED-BUFFERS)). 256 MB is a quarter of the production container's 1 GB limit. |
| `effective_cache_size` | `POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE`, default `768MB` | `4GB` | A planner estimate of shared buffers plus the kernel's cache; it allocates nothing ([query planning](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-query.html#GUC-EFFECTIVE-CACHE-SIZE)). The 4 GB default assumes more memory than the container has, so it is set to the limit minus `shared_buffers`. |
| `work_mem` | `POSTGRES_WORK_MEM`, default `8MB` | `4MB` | Per sort or hash operation, before spilling to temp files. "The total memory used could be many times the value of `work_mem`": several operations per query, several sessions, and hash operations get `hash_mem_multiplier` (2.0) times as much ([resources](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-WORK-MEM)). |
| `maintenance_work_mem` | `POSTGRES_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM`, default `64MB` | `64MB` | `VACUUM`, `CREATE INDEX`, `ALTER TABLE ADD FOREIGN KEY`; each autovacuum worker uses it too, because `autovacuum_work_mem` defaults to -1 ([resources](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-MAINTENANCE-WORK-MEM)). Explicit so it moves with the other three. |
| `log_min_duration_statement` | `500ms` | `-1` (off) | Logs every statement that ran at least that long, the slow-query log ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-MIN-DURATION-STATEMENT)). |
| `log_parameter_max_length` | `0` | `-1` (in full) | Zero "disables logging of bind parameters for non-error statement logs" ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-PARAMETER-MAX-LENGTH)), so a slow-query line never carries a user's values. |
| `log_lock_waits` | `on` | `off` | Logs a session that waits longer than `deadlock_timeout` (1 s) for a lock ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-LOCK-WAITS), [locks](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-locks.html#GUC-DEADLOCK-TIMEOUT)). |
| `log_temp_files` | `0` | `-1` (off) | Logs every temporary file with its size ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-TEMP-FILES)). A spill means a query outgrew `work_mem`. |
| `log_autovacuum_min_duration` | `1s` | `10min` | Logs every autovacuum action that took 1 s or more, and any that was skipped for a lock ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-AUTOVACUUM-MIN-DURATION)). |
| `log_line_prefix` | `%m [%p] %q%u@%d app=%a sqlstate=%e query_id=%Q ` | `%m [%p] ` | Time, process, then user, database, application name (the API connects as `<service name>-api`), SQLSTATE and the query id, which matches `pg_stat_statements.queryid`. `%q` stops background processes before the session-only fields ([logging](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-logging.html#GUC-LOG-LINE-PREFIX)). |
| `archive_mode` | `POSTGRES_ARCHIVE_MODE`, `off` locally, `on` in production | `off` | `deploy/deploy.sh` and the backup scripts export `POSTGRES_ARCHIVE_MODE=on`; local and e2e stacks have nowhere to archive to. |
| `archive_timeout` | `60` | off | Forces a segment switch after a minute with activity, so a lost disk loses about a minute: "`archive_timeout` settings of a minute or so are usually reasonable" ([WAL](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-ARCHIVE-TIMEOUT)). |
| `archive_command`, `restore_command` | `wal-g wal-push %p`, `wal-g wal-fetch %f %p` | empty | Only run when `archive_mode` is on, and during recovery. The local image has no `wal-g`, and it never needs one. |

The service also sets `shm_size: 128mb`, as the postgres image's own compose example does ([postgres image](https://hub.docker.com/_/postgres)): parallel queries share memory through `/dev/shm`.

`docker compose logs postgres` shows the slow-query, lock-wait, temp-file and autovacuum lines. `pg_stat_statements` ranks statements by total time:

```sql
SELECT query, calls, total_exec_time, mean_exec_time FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 5;
```

### Memory limits

Docker sets no memory limit by default ([resource constraints](https://docs.docker.com/engine/containers/resource_constraints/)), so `deploy/docker-compose.production.yml` sets `mem_limit` on every service. `api` and `migrate` get 512 MB each. `postgres` gets `POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT` (default `1g`), which has to hold:

- `shared_buffers`, 256 MB, allocated at start;
- `/dev/shm`, up to 128 MB, which counts against the container like any tmpfs;
- three autovacuum workers (the default `autovacuum_max_workers`, [autovacuum](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-autovacuum.html#GUC-AUTOVACUUM-MAX-WORKERS)) at `maintenance_work_mem` each, 192 MB;
- the API's 15 connections, each with one or two `work_mem` operations at a time, about 240 MB.

That adds up to about 816 MB, and the rest is headroom for connection overhead and the page cache, which the kernel shrinks before it kills anything. Keep `shared_buffers` at a quarter of the limit: raise `POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT` and `POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS` together, and `POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE` to the limit minus `shared_buffers`.

### Backups

Production archives every WAL segment and takes a full base backup each day, with [WAL-G](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g), into any S3-compatible bucket. A restore replays the newest base backup plus the archived WAL to the last archived minute. A scheduled `pg_dump` can't do that: dumps "cannot be used as part of a continuous-archiving solution" ([continuous archiving](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html)), and "except in simple cases, pg_dump is generally not the right choice for taking regular backups of production databases" ([pg_dump](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgdump.html)). WAL-G is the one tool for both halves: it pushes each segment from `archive_command`, takes base backups, encrypts on the client with libsodium, and speaks the S3 API to any store that takes `AWS_ENDPOINT` and path-style addressing ([storages](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g/blob/v3.0.9/docs/STORAGES.md)).

- **Image:** `deploy/postgres/Dockerfile` is `postgres:18-alpine`, the same base as local runs (so the data volume and its collation carry over), plus WAL-G built from the `v3.0.9` tag, checked against its commit. WAL-G's releases are Ubuntu glibc builds and Alpine packages none, so it is built with `-tags libsodium` ([building](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g/blob/v3.0.9/docs/README.md)).
- **Settings and secrets:** `deploy/postgres/backup.env.example` lists the keys. The real file lives outside the checkout with mode 600, and `{{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE` names it; `deploy/deploy.sh` refuses to run when it is missing or has another mode. Compose hands it to the production `postgres` container as `env_file:`, because `archive_command` runs inside that container. No credential is ever a command argument. Anyone who can run `docker inspect` can read it, and that is already root on the host.
- **The key:** `WALG_LIBSODIUM_KEY` is 32 random bytes in hex. Write it straight into the file (`printf 'WALG_LIBSODIUM_KEY=%s\n' "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> <file>`) and copy it somewhere off the host and away from the bucket. Without it no backup can be read, and it can't be replaced in place: old backups need the old key.
- **Bucket rights:** read, write and list. Nothing here deletes, so retention is a lifecycle rule on the bucket, longer than the restore window you promise. Expiring by age is safe because a base backup is taken every day. `WALG_PREVENT_WAL_OVERWRITE` makes a retried push compare with the archived segment instead of overwriting it ([PostgreSQL](https://github.com/wal-g/wal-g/blob/v3.0.9/docs/PostgreSQL.md)), so a bucket with object lock works too.
- **`deploy/postgres/backup_postgres.sh`** checks that `archive_mode` is on, writes a restore point, switches the WAL segment and waits until `pg_stat_archiver` reports it archived, runs `wal-g backup-push`, then `wal-g wal-verify integrity timeline`, and fails on any lost segment. `deploy/deploy.sh` runs it after every deploy, so the first deploy has a base backup and every migration has a fresh one behind it.
- **`deploy/postgres/verify_postgres_backup_restore.sh`** is the restore drill. It refuses when the newest base backup is older than 26 hours. It counts every table in production, switches the WAL segment and waits for the archive, then starts a throwaway container from the production image on `backend_egress`, the network the archiver reaches the bucket through, with no published port. It runs `wal-g backup-fetch LATEST`, replays the archived WAL with `recovery.signal` and `restore_command`, and promotes ([recovery](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html#BACKUP-PITR-RECOVERY)). The copy runs with `archive_mode=off`, so it never pushes into the production prefix. It passes when the copy is out of recovery, its Alembic revision equals production's, and every table's row count is within `POSTGRES_RESTORE_DRILL_ROW_SLACK` (default 10) of production's at the switch. It prints the seconds to a usable database and the last replayed commit, then removes the container and its volume. Production only gets reads, one restore point and one WAL switch.

Restore after losing the host: put back the checkout, the deploy `.env`, the database owner env file and the backup env file with the key, then

```sh
export {{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<deploy .env> {{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<database owner env file> \
  {{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<backup env file> POSTGRES_ARCHIVE_MODE=on
compose=(docker compose --env-file "${{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE" -f docker-compose.yml -f deploy/docker-compose.production.yml)
"${compose[@]}" build postgres
"${compose[@]}" run --rm --no-deps --user postgres --entrypoint sh postgres -c \
  'mkdir -p -m 0700 "$PGDATA" && wal-g backup-fetch "$PGDATA" LATEST && touch "$PGDATA/recovery.signal"'
deploy/deploy.sh
```

Postgres replays every archived segment and then promotes to a new timeline. A long replay can outlast `--wait-timeout`; when `deploy.sh` gives up, wait for "archive recovery complete" in `docker compose logs postgres` and run it again.

### Backup schedule

WAL archiving runs inside Postgres and needs no schedule. The base backups and the drill run from `deploy/postgres/run_postgres_backup_schedule.sh`, a plain loop: a base backup every `POSTGRES_BACKUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (default a day), the drill every `POSTGRES_RESTORE_DRILL_INTERVAL_SECONDS` (default a week), a retry after `POSTGRES_BACKUP_RETRY_SECONDS` (default an hour) when either fails, and one UTC-stamped line per run. It has no cron entry or systemd timer. Start it in its own tmux server so it outlives the login shell:

```sh
systemd-run --scope --unit=tmux-{{ project_slug }} tmux -L {{ project_slug }} new-session -d -s postgres-backups \
  -e {{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<deploy .env> -e {{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<database owner env file> \
  -e {{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE=<backup env file> \
  "deploy/postgres/run_postgres_backup_schedule.sh 2>&1 | tee -a <log file>"
```

Both scripts take a lock, so a run by hand and the loop never overlap. A host reboot ends the loop; start it again, and its first pass runs at once, so it catches up. When it stays down, the drill run by hand fails on the base backup's age, and the next deploy still takes a base backup.
{%- endif %}

## Connection packages and external HTTP APIs

A connection package owns one external system's configuration and client:

```
app/infrastructure/<name>_api/
  configuration.py   base URL, key or credentials (SecretStr), timeouts
  client.py          <Name>ApiClient: one method per endpoint, typed arguments, returns models
  schemas.py         request and response bodies as Pydantic models
  errors.py          <Name>ApiError and the few cases callers handle (NotFound, RateLimited)
```

- **One `httpx.AsyncClient` per API per process**, opened on the process's `AsyncExitStack`, with `base_url`, default headers and an explicit `httpx.Timeout`.
- **The client method is the contract.** It maps statuses to exceptions and returns `Model.model_validate(response.json())`. Callers never see `httpx.Response` or raw dicts.
- **Retries** with [stamina](https://stamina.hynek.me/en/stable/), only on connection errors, 429 and 5xx, only for requests safe to repeat, honouring `Retry-After`. `httpx.AsyncHTTPTransport(retries=n)` retries only connect failures ([transports](https://www.python-httpx.org/advanced/transports/)).
- **Credentials live in the client**, never in a caller: a static key is a default header; an expiring token is refreshed by one shared task, so parallel callers never refresh twice ([RFC 9700 §4.14.2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700#section-4.14.2)).

{% if include_postgres %}`models.py` means SQLAlchemy tables everywhere and `schemas.py`{% else %}`schemas.py`{% endif %} means Pydantic wire shapes everywhere, so an API package's bodies are `schemas.py`.

## Configuration

Every setting lives in a pydantic-settings `BaseSettings` in a `configuration.py`: one per package that has settings, each with its own `env_prefix` (`{{ environment_prefix }}_` for the app, `{{ environment_prefix }}_<PACKAGE>_` for a package{% if include_accounts %}, such as `{{ environment_prefix }}_ACCOUNTS_`{% endif %}). App-wide settings (service identity, {% if include_postgres %}the database host, port and passwords, {% endif %}trusted proxy addresses, the log level) go in `app/configuration.py`.

- **Non-secret settings carry their default in the class**; the environment only overrides it.
- **Secrets have no default**, are `SecretStr`{% if include_postgres %} with `repr=False`{% endif %}, and a missing one fails at startup.
{%- if include_postgres %}
- **Each password is set once.** No env file holds a DSN: `ConnectionSettings.build_application_database_url()` and `MigrationConnectionSettings.build_migration_database_url()` build it with `PostgresDsn.build` from the password, the role and database names from the template answers, the `postgresql+psycopg` driver, and `{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_HOST` (default `postgres`) and `{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_PORT` (default 5432), and return it as `SecretStr`. The password is percent-encoded, so any character works.
{%- endif %}
- **Each getter is `@lru_cache`** ([FastAPI settings](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/settings/#creating-the-settings-only-once-with-lru-cache)).
- **Services receive settings values by keyword** from the lifespan or a dependency{% if include_accounts %} (`AccountService.on_session(session, relying_party=...)`){% endif %}; they never read settings themselves.
- **Nothing else reads the environment.** ruff's banned-api refuses `os.environ` and `os.getenv` everywhere{% if include_postgres %}, and `migrations/env.py` builds its DSN from `read_migration_connection_settings()`{% endif %}.
{%- if include_postgres %}
- **The example env files list every key**, and they are the only other place a key name appears. Compose repeats no key in `environment:`. Each secret reaches only the containers that use it:
  - `.env.example` becomes the API's env file (`{{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, default `.env`), handed to `api` and `migrate` through `env_file:`. It holds `{{ environment_prefix }}_APPLICATION_DATABASE_PASSWORD`, the application role's password (`ConnectionSettings`), and also feeds compose interpolation.
  - `.env.database-owner.example` becomes the database owner env file (`{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, default `.env.database-owner`), handed only to `postgres` and `migrate`. It holds `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, read by the image and by `migrate` (`MigrationConnectionSettings`), which also reads the application role's password from the API's env file to set it. The API never gets the superuser password.
{%- else %}
- **`.env.example` lists every key**, and it is the only other place a key name appears. Compose hands the env file to the backend containers through `env_file:` (`{{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, default `.env`) and repeats no key in `environment:`.
{%- endif %}

## One resources lifespan

`app/lifespan/resources.py` holds `Resources`, a frozen, typed model, and each process role opens it once with an `AsyncExitStack` (`app/lifespan/composition.py`), so resources close in reverse order even when startup fails halfway. A missing or wrong resource fails at startup, before the first request{% if include_postgres %}: the database refuses a schema behind Alembic head{% endif %}. Dependencies read it through one typed accessor (`ResourcesDependency`); nothing else reads `app.state`.

## Startup does no work

The API is built by a uvicorn factory (`create_app`, run with `factory=True` by `python -m app serve-api`), so importing `app.main` reads no settings. The API lifespan opens connections and returns. {% if include_postgres %}Migrations, seeding, rebuilding derived state and maintenance are deploy steps (the compose `migrate` service) or worker passes{% else %}Seeding, rebuilding derived state and maintenance are deploy steps or worker passes{% endif %}, so a restart is fast and the same everywhere.

## Background work

Work outside a request runs in a worker process, never inside the web server. The template ships none; an app that needs one adds `python -m app run-worker` as its own compose service from the same image. The worker opens `Resources` like the API and runs each job as a plain `async` loop: one pass{% if include_postgres %}, one transaction{% endif %}, then a sleep, all in one `asyncio.TaskGroup`. A pass that fails is logged and the loop goes on; a loop that dies is logged and ends the process, so compose restarts it.

{%- if include_postgres %}
- **One active worker.** The worker takes a Postgres session advisory lock (`pg_try_advisory_lock`) on startup; a second worker waits and does nothing until the lock frees.
{%- else %}
- **One active worker.** Compose runs one replica of the worker service.
{%- endif %}
- **Every pass is idempotent**: it can run twice or be killed halfway without damage. A pass that sends something records the send first.
- **Passes read current data.** A pass loads what it needs each time, so a restarted worker picks up where {% if include_postgres %}the database says{% else %}the systems it reads say{% endif %}.
- **Shutdown:** SIGTERM cancels the loops, {% if include_postgres %}the passes roll back, {% endif %}and `Resources` closes. `stop_grace_period` is longer than the slowest pass.

{%- if include_postgres %}

There is no job queue library. When work must be deferred from a request, the request writes a row in its own transaction and a worker loop picks it up, so the work exists only if the write committed.

## Events and webhooks

A change and the message about it must not drift apart. Use a transactional outbox: write the event in the same transaction as the change, and let a worker send it, so a message is sent "if and only if the database transaction commits"; the consumer must be idempotent ([transactional outbox](https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox.html)).

Receiving a webhook: verify the signature against the raw body and reject old timestamps ([Standard Webhooks](https://www.standardwebhooks.com/)), store the event id and skip repeats, record the work and return `2xx` at once, and never rely on arrival order.
{%- endif %}

## CLI

One entry point, `python -m app` (and `{{ project_slug }}` in `[project.scripts]`), built on typer. Each process role is a command (`serve-api`, and `run-worker` once there is one). Each domain's commands live in its `cli.py`, added to `app/cli.py`. A command opens only the resources it needs and calls a service.

{%- if include_postgres %}

## Migrations

Alembic owns the schema: one linear history in `backend/migrations/versions/` (`NNNN_<name>.py`), no `create_all`, no hand `ALTER` on a live database. After a model change: `alembic revision --autogenerate`, read the file, then `alembic check` must report nothing.

An applied revision never changes; a fix is the next revision. Forward only: `downgrade()` raises. `create index concurrently` goes in its own revision with `autocommit_block()`. A change to an existing column is expand, then contract once every caller has moved. A new or renamed Postgres enum member is a revision too (`ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE` or `RENAME VALUE`). So is a Postgres extension: `0002` creates `pg_stat_statements`.
{%- endif %}

## Verification

No unit tests. Verification is running the real system: the flows in `e2e/` start the compose stack and check it from outside. Day to day, the gates below are what runs.

- **A flow is one user journey**, start to finish, through the public HTTP surface. It is named `<journey>_flow.py`{% if include_accounts %}, like `accounts_flow.py`: sign up with a passkey, sign out, sign in again{% endif %}.
- **No file per endpoint, per operation or per query, and no SQL fixture files.** They pin the schema and the implementation, so every migration breaks them. An upsert, for example, is checked inside the journey that submits twice.
- **Shared code lives in modules named by concern** (`stack_commands.py` starts and stops the stack, `http_client.py` sends requests, `flow_report.py` prints the results{% if include_accounts %}, `software_passkey.py` signs like an authenticator{% endif %}). A flow never copies code from another flow.
- **State comes in through the API**, never through SQL inserts.
- **Assertions go through the API.** A `psql` check through `docker compose exec` is allowed only for an invariant the API can't show, such as a row really deleted or a role lacking a privilege. It runs in a read-only transaction and never writes.
- **Each flow starts its own stack** with fresh secrets on a free port, prints one `PASS` or `FAIL` line per assertion, and exits 0 only when all of them pass. It removes the stack and its volumes even after a failure. Run output goes outside the repository.
- **When a flow is due:** after a change to its journey's behaviour, a migration, the compose files or the deploy files. Flows run by hand, never as a CI or hook gate.
- **A flow that only pinned old details** gets deleted or rewritten when a refactor breaks it, never patched around.

## HTTP API conventions

- **One error shape.** Every error leaves as `{"error": {"code", "message", "details", "parameters", "request_id"}}`; the last three are present only when they carry something. The status is the class and `code` is the cause: a stable, flat `snake_case` name a client branches on, never renamed or reused. `message` is English for developers and a safe fallback; a client shows its own words, keyed by `code`. A refusal is an `AppError` subclass with a `code`, an HTTP status and a safe message. A value the message mentions also travels typed in `parameters`, a frozen `ErrorParameters` model the class names as `parameters_model` (`passkey_credential_id_too_long` carries `maximum_bytes`), so no client parses it out of English. No stack trace, SQL, exception text or upstream error text ever leaves. `app/shared/exception_handlers.py` and `app/unhandled_error_middleware.py` turn every exception into the envelope.
- **Validation** answers 400 `validation_error` with every field error in `details`, each `{code, pointer}`. `pointer` is an [RFC 6901](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901.html) JSON Pointer whose first segment names the location: `/body/credential/id`, `/query/limit`, `/path/account_id`. `code` is an `ErrorDetailCode` (`required`, `wrong_type`, `too_long` and so on) mapped from pydantic's error type. The rejected input, pydantic's `ctx` and a validator's own text never leave. A body that is not JSON answers 400 `malformed_body`. Rate limits answer 429 `rate_limited` with `Retry-After`.
- **Correlation id.** Every response carries a server-minted `X-Request-ID`. A 5xx body repeats it as `request_id` so a user can quote it, and the server log holds the stack trace under the same id.
- **The OpenAPI document is the contract.** It is committed at `backend/openapi.json`, and `uv run python tools/dump_openapi.py openapi.json` rewrites it, so every API change shows up in review as a diff of that file. Publish the reference from this file, never from the running app: `/docs` and `/openapi.json` are off. Each error status of an operation lists its codes in `x-error-codes`, and `x-error-parameters` gives the schema of each code that carries values. Both are generated from the `AppError` classes the route names in `responses=describe_error_responses((...))`, plus the shared `validation_error`, `malformed_body`, `rate_limited` and `internal_error`. `default` is the shared `ApiError` response, and every response documents `X-Request-ID`. A route that starts raising a new refusal names its class there. A new code on an existing operation is a contract change, so clients fall back on the status class for a code they don't know. Clients are built from the document.
- **Pagination** by cursor for lists that grow; offset only for small, bounded lists.
- **Idempotency keys** on POST routes a client may retry.
- **Every route has a typed return annotation**, which FastAPI uses as its response model.
- **Minimal payloads with consistent names.** A field has one name across REST, events and stored payloads.
- **Request budgets** are an in-memory GCRA in the API process (`app/infrastructure/rate_limit/`, `app/rate_limit_middleware.py`), per client IP. One API replica only: a second replica would double every budget.
- **Client IP** comes from `X-Real-IP` only when the peer is one of `{{ environment_prefix }}_TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESSES`.

## Python and async

**Python 3.15.** One version everywhere: `.python-version`, `requires-python`, ruff's `target-version`, ty and the Dockerfile `FROM` lines, bumped together in one change.

**Async all the way.** Every I/O path is `async`. A library that only blocks is wrapped once, in its infrastructure package, and runs on its own named `BlockingExecutor` (see Runtime defaults). Ruff's `ASYNC` rules catch blocking calls inside `async def`.

- **Parallel calls** go through `asyncio.TaskGroup`: when one fails, the others are cancelled. `asyncio.gather` is banned.
- **Deadlines** use `async with asyncio.timeout(...)` around the whole operation. `asyncio.wait_for` is banned.
- **Fire-and-forget** only for best-effort work, keeping a reference to each task: the event loop "only keeps weak references to tasks" ([asyncio tasks](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-task.html)).
- **CPU-heavy work** runs in a process pool or a worker, never on the event loop.

## Runtime defaults

- **HTTP JSON:** no custom response class. With a typed return, FastAPI serializes through Pydantic straight to bytes, and it deprecates `ORJSONResponse` and `UJSONResponse` ([custom response](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/custom-response/#orjson-or-response-model)). Both are on banned-api.
- **JSON elsewhere:** Pydantic's `model_validate_json`, `model_dump_json` and `TypeAdapter`. Raw JSON with no model uses [orjson](https://github.com/ijl/orjson) (`tools/dump_openapi.py`). The standard library `json` is on banned-api.
- **Event loop:** [uvloop](https://pypi.org/project/uvloop/). The API runs under uvicorn with `loop="uvloop"` (`app/cli.py`), and every other async entry point starts with `uvloop.run(main())`{% if include_postgres %} (`migrations/env.py`){% endif %}. Event-loop policies are deprecated since Python 3.14 ([asyncio policies](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-policy.html)), so `asyncio.run`, `asyncio.set_event_loop_policy` and `uvloop.install` are on banned-api.
- **Blocking work** runs on a named `BlockingExecutor` per kind of work{% if include_postgres %} (`app/shared/blocking_executor.py`){% endif %}, opened on the process's `AsyncExitStack` and held on `Resources`{% if include_postgres %} (`file_read_executor` reads the Alembic head at startup){% else %}; the app has no blocking work yet, so it has no executor{% endif %}. The default executor also serves the loop's DNS lookups, so one slow blocking call there stalls name resolution for the whole process. `asyncio.to_thread` is on banned-api.
- **`lazy import`** ([PEP 810](https://peps.python.org/pep-0810/)) for a heavy module a CLI command or worker only sometimes needs. The template has none yet.

## Gates

One command runs every gate: `uvx prek run --all-files` ([prek](https://github.com/j178/prek) reads `.pre-commit-config.yaml`; the hooks are `repo: local` with `language: system`, so tool versions come from `backend/uv.lock`). Copier installs it as a pre-commit and pre-push hook (`uvx prek install`).

- **uv:** `uv.lock` is committed; the hooks and the image install with `--locked`. `prerelease = "explicit"`, and `exclude-newer = "7 days"` keeps any release younger than a week out of the lock, so a bad upload has time to be caught before an app pulls it ([uv resolution](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/resolution/)).
- **ruff format and ruff check** (`app tools{% if include_postgres %} migrations{% endif %}`): `ALL` (dead code through `F401`, `F841` and `ARG`; complexity through `C901` at `max-complexity = 10`, `PLR0911`, `PLR0912` and `PLR0915`), with `D`, `CPY` and `COM812` off, and `TC001`-`TC003` off because FastAPI and pydantic read annotations at runtime, so type-only imports break routes. Its banned-api list (`[tool.ruff.lint.flake8-tidy-imports.banned-api]`) refuses `typing.cast`, `typing.Any`, `os.environ`, `os.getenv`, `json`, dataclasses, NamedTuple bundles, `requests`, `aiohttp`, `asyncio.gather`, `asyncio.wait_for`, `asyncio.to_thread`, `asyncio.run`, event-loop policies, hand-made decorators, `fastapi.HTTPException`, custom JSON response classes and loggers other than `app.shared.logging`.
- **ty** (`app tools`, pinned exactly; it is pre-1.0), with the possibly-* rules as errors and `respect-type-ignore-comments = false`.
- **semgrep** (`semgrep/house-rules.yaml`, over `app`{% if include_postgres %} and `migrations`{% endif %}, pinned 1.183.0; the hook fails on any finding and on any parse error in its JSON report, because a file semgrep cannot parse, such as one with `lazy import`, is otherwise skipped silently): the structural rules ruff cannot express: typed log emission, no type dispatch on our own classes, no `isinstance` parsing of JSON, no `model_copy(update=)`, no `from_attributes`, no `include=`/`exclude=` on wire output, `app.state` only through `Resources`, services never keep `Resources`, no underscore-private methods, no `@staticmethod`, no exception text copied into a raised error, closed sets are enums, wire model naming.
- **lint-imports:** the `layers` contract, an `acyclic_siblings` contract over `app.{{ root_package }}`{% if include_accounts %}, the `forbidden` contracts (routes and entry modules never import a model or repository; models never import FastAPI or Starlette) and one `protected` contract per domain (only `app.{{ root_package }}.accounts` imports its models and repository){% endif %}. A new domain adds its own `protected` contract. Each contract carries `broken_contract_guidance` saying how to fix a break.
- **deptry:** every dependency is used and every import is declared.
- **OpenAPI:** the hook rewrites `backend/openapi.json` from the code and [openapi-spec-validator](https://github.com/python-openapi/openapi-spec-validator) checks it as OpenAPI 3.1. A route change that is not reflected in the committed file fails the hook, because the hook leaves the file modified.
- **e2e fast checks:** ruff format, ruff check and ty over `e2e/`.
- **gitleaks** over the git history.
- **uv audit** (pre-push and `manual` only, because it needs the network): checks every locked backend dependency against the [OSV](https://osv.dev/) database. It exits 1 on a known vulnerability and 2 when it cannot reach the service, so a finding and an outage read differently. It is still marked experimental in uv. When a fix is younger than the 7-day `exclude-newer` cooldown, add an `exclude-newer-package` entry for that one package in `backend/pyproject.toml`, run `uv lock --upgrade-package <name>`, and delete the entry once the cooldown has passed ([uv resolution](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/concepts/resolution/)). Run it by hand with `uvx prek run --hook-stage manual backend-audit`.
{%- if include_postgres %}

`alembic check` needs a database, so it is not a hook: after a model change, run `alembic revision --autogenerate` against a running stack, read the file, then `alembic check` must report nothing. The shell scripts in `deploy/` have no hook either; run `shellcheck` on them after a change.
{%- endif %}

## Container image

Multi-stage, following [uv's Docker guide](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/guides/integration/docker/). Dependencies install before the source is copied, so a code change reuses the dependency layer. The virtualenv is built in a builder stage and copied whole, so a Python bump changes only the `FROM` lines. The container runs as a non-root user, read-only with a small `/tmp`, all capabilities dropped, and starts the venv's binaries directly. One image serves every process (`serve-api`{% if include_postgres %}, `alembic upgrade head`{% endif %}, a worker). `Dockerfile.dockerignore` excludes everything the service does not run.

## Environments and running it

The app runs as Docker Compose on one host: `api`{% if include_postgres %}, `migrate`, `postgres`{% endif %}, behind a reverse proxy (`deploy/Caddyfile`). One image moves between environments; only environment variables differ, and every environment has its own {% if include_postgres %}database and {% endif %}credentials. `deploy/deploy.sh` builds the image from the checkout and starts the stack with `deploy/docker-compose.production.yml`, which joins the reverse proxy's network and sets a `mem_limit` on every service. No rollback: a bad release is fixed forward.
{%- if include_postgres %}

Production differs from a local run in Postgres only: the `postgres` service runs the image from `deploy/postgres/Dockerfile` (the local image plus WAL-G), archives its WAL (`POSTGRES_ARCHIVE_MODE=on`, exported by `deploy/deploy.sh`), reads the backup env file, joins `backend_egress` to reach the bucket, and gets 60 s to stop so the archiver can push what is left. `deploy/deploy.sh` needs three paths in its environment, `{{ environment_prefix }}_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, `{{ environment_prefix }}_DATABASE_OWNER_ENVIRONMENT_FILE` and `{{ environment_prefix }}_POSTGRES_BACKUP_ENVIRONMENT_FILE`, and takes a base backup once the stack is up. Postgres operations has the settings, limits, backups and schedule.
{%- endif %}

Every long-running service has:

- **One health endpoint.** `/health` returns 200 while the process runs and checks nothing else.
- **Graceful shutdown.** On SIGTERM the process stops taking work, finishes what is running, then closes resources. `stop_grace_period` is longer than the slowest request or pass.
- **Limits.** Pools, request body sizes (64 KB at the proxy) and request budgets have explicit maxima.

## Conventions

- Exceptions propagate. Catch only a known error you can name, and turn it into an `AppError` at the boundary. Never forward an internal message to a client.
- No fallback optionality. A parameter is optional only when the domain has no value for it.
- No constants or wrappers without a second caller.
- Models we build ourselves are built with keyword constructors, which ty checks. `model_validate` and `model_validate_json` read data from outside (request bodies, external APIs), never our own rows or objects, so nothing uses `from_attributes`.
- The response bases are `strict=True`: our own outputs are built with the declared types, so a coerced value is a bug ([strict mode](https://pydantic.dev/docs/validation/latest/concepts/strict_mode/)).
- No `model_copy(update=...)`: neither ty nor Pydantic checks the update dict. No call-site `include=` or `exclude=` on wire output: a different shape is a different model, as [FastAPI recommends](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/response-model/#response-model-include-and-response-model-exclude).
- No type dispatch on our own classes: the behaviour lives on the class as a method or property, or Pydantic's discriminator picks the class ([Replace Conditional with Polymorphism](https://refactoring.com/catalog/replaceConditionalWithPolymorphism.html)). An enum's mapping is one property on the enum whose body is a `match` ending in `assert_never`, because ty proves that exhaustive. `assert` is banned (ruff `S101`).
- Grouped values and collaborator carriers are frozen Pydantic models (`ConfigDict(frozen=True)`, plus `arbitrary_types_allowed=True` when fields are clients or services), like `Resources`.
- Everything is UTC: timezone-aware `datetime`{% if include_postgres %}, `timestamptz` columns{% endif %}, `TZ=UTC` in every process.
- No comments or docstrings: a name, a type or a smaller function says it. The two exceptions are the reason on a linter suppression and the link to the upstream bug a workaround exists for.
- No hand-made decorators: the only decorators are the libraries' documented API (`router.get`, typer's `command`, `property`, Pydantic validators, `lru_cache` on settings getters as [FastAPI documents](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/advanced/settings/#creating-the-settings-only-once-with-lru-cache)).
- A change that introduces a pattern moves every caller in the same change. Nothing is left half on the old way.

## Logging

Configured once, at the start of every process (`configure_logging` in `app/shared/logging.py`).

- **Output:** unbuffered stdout, JSON in containers, a readable renderer on a terminal. Tracebacks are rendered with quoted values redacted.
- **Typed events:** `log.emit(logger, event=AccountSignedIn(account_id=...))`. Each event is a `LogEvent` model in the owning package's `log_events.py` with a fixed message and operation; values go in fields, never in the message.
- **Request context:** a server-minted correlation id goes on every line of a request and back in `X-Request-ID`.
- **Standard library logs** go through the same processors, so a process writes one format.
- **Never logged:** secrets, tokens, full request or response bodies, personal data.
- **One line per request**, written by the request log middleware.

## Naming and explicitness

Code reads like a sentence at the call site: `await account_service.get_account_summary(account_id)`, not `await svc.get(a)`.

- **Keyword-only parameters.** A function takes at most one positional subject besides `self`; everything after it is keyword-only (ruff `PLR0917` with `max-positional-args = 1`). Signatures a library fixes are the exception, each with a reason on the suppression.
- **Names are long form, whole words**: `configuration`, not `cfg`; `error`, not `e`.
- **Don't repeat the type** in a name: `accounts`, not `account_list`.
- **Functions are verb plus object** and say what they return: `get_` returns one thing or raises, `find_` returns one thing or `None`, `list_` returns a list, `count_` an int; `insert_`, `delete_` and `send_` change something.
- **Wire models put the thing first and the role last.** A request body ends in `Request`; a response ends in `Response` or names its part.
- **Units in the name** when the type does not carry them: `TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, `max_size_bytes`, `created_at`.
- **Constants** are `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`. A constant shared by two domains belongs to the one that owns its meaning.
- **Closed sets are enums**: no `Literal` with two or more string values.
- **Namespaced keys** everywhere a string is an identifier: log operations, env variables (`{{ environment_prefix }}_<SETTING>`), cookie names.

## Using the libraries fully

Before writing a helper, check whether the library already does it.

- **FastAPI:** dependencies as `Annotated` aliases, a typed return on every route and no `response_model=` that repeats it (ruff `FAST`), `fastapi.status` constants, `lifespan=`, `APIRouter(prefix=..., tags=[...])` per domain.
- **Pydantic:** constraints in the type (`Field(gt=0, max_length=200)`, `SecretStr`), `ConfigDict(frozen=True)` for values that must not change, `TypeAdapter` for plain types, `JsonValue` for arbitrary JSON, `model_validate_json` and `model_dump(mode="json")` at the boundaries, discriminated unions instead of type checks.
{%- if include_postgres %}
- **SQLAlchemy:** `select()` with explicit columns for partial reads, `with_for_update()` for locked reads, bulk `insert().values([...])`, never string-formatted SQL.
{%- endif %}
- **httpx:** `base_url`, default headers, `httpx.Timeout`, `httpx.Auth`.
- **Standard library:** `enum.StrEnum`, `datetime.UTC`, `pathlib.Path`, `asyncio.TaskGroup` and `asyncio.timeout`, `itertools.batched`.

## Reliability habits

- **Fail fast at startup.** Settings validate on load, `Resources` opens every connection before the server accepts traffic, and required settings have no defaults.
- **Timeouts everywhere:** HTTP clients, {% if include_postgres %}`statement_timeout`, `lock_timeout`, idle transactions, {% endif %}each worker pass, and shutdown drains.
- **Bounded retries** with backoff and jitter, only for errors a retry can fix.
- **Degrade, don't fall over.** When an optional integration is down, the request still works and the failure is logged.

## Starting a new domain

{%- if include_postgres %}
1. Pick its place in the import-linter `layers` contract; it may import only what sits below it. Add its `protected` contract.
2. Create `app/{{ root_package }}/<domain>/models.py`, `repository.py`, `service.py`, and `types.py` if other domains read it.
3. Import its `models` in `migrations/env.py` and add an Alembic revision for its tables.
4. Add `configuration.py` with its own `env_prefix`, and its keys in `.env.example`, if it has settings.
5. Add `router.py`, `schemas.py` and `dependencies.py` only when HTTP needs them, `sweeps.py` when a worker runs something for it, `cli.py` when an operator needs it.
6. Add its wiring in `app/lifespan/`.
{%- else %}
1. Pick its place in the import-linter `layers` contract; it may import only what sits below it.
2. Create `app/{{ root_package }}/<domain>/service.py`, and `types.py` if other domains read it.
3. Add `configuration.py` with its own `env_prefix`, and its keys in `.env.example`, if it has settings.
4. Add `router.py`, `schemas.py` and `dependencies.py` only when HTTP needs them, `sweeps.py` when a worker runs something for it, `cli.py` when an operator needs it.
5. Add its wiring in `app/lifespan/`.
{%- endif %}
